026-04-12T17:49:23Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "Vats: 23, Sal: 12, Nikhil: 10", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-12T17:50:17Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "I am really happy with my random choice of April 12 to do QOTD. A lot of things that interest me happened on this day. Of all of them, this next one is my favorite…", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-12T17:50:36Z", "username": "Abhishek Vats", "text": "Kharoshti 🙈", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-12T17:50:43Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "4.\tIt was Good Friday on April 12, 1963 when X was arrested for violating a court injunction against “parading, demonstrating, boycotting, trespassing, and picketing.” In solitary confinement, he was handed a newspaper that had been smuggled inside containing a statement by eight clergymen titled “A Call for Unity.” That statement called X’s protests “unwise and untimely.” Using the margins of the same newspaper, and a legal pad from his lawyer, X composed Y, his response to that statement that begins with the words, “my dear fellow clergymen.” Y, also known as “The _____ Is Your Brother,” says that people have a moral responsibility to break unjust laws and to take direct action rather than wait potentially forever for justice come through the courts. Y, at least to me, is one of the most important documents in American history. It contains the famous truism: “injustice anywhere is a threat to justice everywhere.” ID X (5 points) and Y (10 points).", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-12T17:50:44Z", "username": "Khandoba", "text": "Gandhi was not born on this day", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-12T17:50:58